Objective

Learn how Microsoft Entra ID uses Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to give administrators only the permissions required for their responsibilities.

Environment

* Microsoft 365 Business Standard trial
* Microsoft Entra ID
* Test users:
    * Test Licensing
    * Bob John
    * Sarah Williams
* Test role:
    * Helpdesk Administrator

Practical

I created a dedicated Test Licensing account to simulate a helpdesk technician rather than using the global administrator account for every task.

I assigned:

Helpdesk Administrator

to the Test Licensing account.
The role appeared under:
Entra ID
→ Users
→ Test Licensing
→ Assigned roles
→ Directory roles

The assignment was:
Role: Helpdesk Administrator
Assignment: Direct
Role type: Built-in
Resource: Directory

Permission Testing

I signed into Microsoft Entra as the Test Licensing technician account and tested operations against Bob John.

Password Reset

The technician could access:

Bob John → Reset password

This demonstrated that the Helpdesk Administrator role can perform password-reset tasks for appropriate non-administrator users.

I did not complete the reset because the test incident was related to MFA rather than a forgotten password.

Revoke Sessions

The technician was also able to use:

Bob John → Revoke sessions

The operation successfully revoked Bob’s active sign-in sessions.

This demonstrated a useful security response for situations such as a suspected compromised or lost-device scenario.

Authentication Methods

When the technician opened:

Bob John → Authentication methods

the portal displayed:

“You do not have access to this data.”

This was an important practical discovery.

The technician’s role did not provide the required visibility for managing Bob’s authentication-method information in this tenant.

Key Lesson

Different administrative tasks require different permissions.
For example:
Password reset
       ↓
Helpdesk Administrator.

while authentication-method administration may require a more specific authentication-management role.

Least Privilege Principle

Instead of giving every IT employee Global Administrator access:
Global Administrator
        ↓
Everything

we aim for:
Helpdesk Technician
        ↓
Only the permissions required.
This reduces unnecessary administrative access and limits the potential impact of account compromise.




